detail.abstract:
Background: Nigeria's pharmaceutical marketthe largest in sub-Saharan Africaimports over 70% of its medicines, creating structural vulnerability to counterfeit drug infiltration. An estimated 10-30% of medicines in circulation are substandard or falsified. This crisis is responsible for an estimated 64,000 preventable deaths annually in Nigeria from counterfeit antimalarial drugs alone, generates $2 billion in annual economic losses across the continent, and accelerates antimicrobial resistance (AMR)a threat projected to cause 10 million deaths globally per year by 2050.&nbsp;
<div>
 Objective: To develop, model, and evaluate a Multidisciplinary Pharmaceutical Security Framework (MPSF) integrating artificial intelligence (AI), blockchain traceability, pharmaceutical microbiology, and ethical governance as a systemic, evidence-based response to counterfeit drug infiltration in Nigeria's cross-border pharmaceutical supply chain.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Methods: Systematic literature review (PRISMA), comparative case analysis across five implementation contexts (Ghana, India, USA, Southeast Asia, China), quantitative simulation modelling using validated effectiveness parameters, and principlist bioethics analysis. Mathematical models are developed for counterfeit risk scoring, supply chain integrity indexing, and MPSF impact quantification.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: Full MPSF implementation is projected to achieve a 60% reduction in counterfeit drug infiltration within 36 months, preventing approximately 38,400 deaths annually and generating ₦25 billion in annual financial savings. AI-powered border screening projects a 35-47% interception rate. Blockchain serialisation projects a 30-40% reduction in post-manufacture tampering. Pharmaceutical microbiology adds a 15% incremental interception layer. Integration of all four MPSF components achieves compound effectiveness exceeding the sum of individual components due to interdependence effects quantified through the MPSF Integration Multiplier (IM = 1.34).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusions: The MPSF represents the most comprehensive evidence-based framework for pharmaceutical supply chain security available for Nigeria's regulatory context. It is directly crossreferenced with the Verified Trade Infrastructure (VTI) framework developed in Ani (2025, SSRN WP-2025-001), which established the foundational architecture for AI-powered supply chain verification applicable to pharmaceutical trade corridors. Immediate phased deployment is recommended beginning with Nigeria's five major pharmaceutical import entry points.
</div>
